"use client";

import { useId, useRef, useState } from "react";

/**
 * Adamastor seal (footer colophon) — tinted rainbow rising from the sea (Easter egg).
 *
 * Brand context: Adamastor is the sea monster from Portuguese mythology
 * (Os Lusíadas — guardian of the Cape of Storms). The wave lines at the
 * bottom of the mark depict the sea. This animation maps the bloom to
 * that metaphor: a TINTED rainbow rises out of the waves (pink → peach
 * → cream → mint → sky → lavender → pink) and washes UPWARD through
 * Adamastor's body. Every stop is a tint (high L, moderate C) so the
 * rainbow reads playful but on-brand, not chaotic — and the upward
 * direction ties the motion to the brand metaphor.
 *
 * At rest: static, filled in the cascaded theme color (text-navy /
 * text-white). Cursor:pointer is the only invitation — discovery is
 * half the delight.
 *
 * Hover signal (so users discover this is interactive):
 *   - cursor:pointer (baseline for all users)
 *   - gentle scale-105 (motion-safe only — subtle 5% lift signals "this
 *     responds to you" without competing with footer content)
 *   - PERSISTENCE: scale-105 holds for the full click animation duration
 *     (via the `active` state), so the seal stays lifted while the
 *     rainbow plays even if the cursor moves away. After the animation
 *     ends and `active` flips false, scale eases back to 1 unless the
 *     cursor is still over it (in which case hover keeps it lifted).
 *
 * On click: paths swap fill from `currentColor` to a vertical
 * `<linearGradient>` with TWO key tricks:
 *
 *   1. `spreadMethod="repeat"` + wide vertical gradient (y1=1 at the
 *      sea-line, y2=-0.2 above the head): cycle length = 1.2 BBox
 *      units. The shape (1 unit tall) shows roughly 5-6 stops at any
 *      moment — bigger bands than a narrow gradient. The palette
 *      still tiles vertically so cycles continue passing through over
 *      time.
 *
 *   2. Continuous upward slide via `<animateTransform>`: the gradient
 *      translates from (0,0) to (0, -4.8) over 3 seconds at constant
 *      speed. Negative y in SVG = upward, so the pattern rises. With
 *      the gradient tiled at 1.2 BBox units per cycle, that means ~4
 *      full color cycles flow up through the seal — colors emerging
 *      from below the sea line and washing upward through Adamastor.
 *      `fill="freeze"` holds the end transform until the fill swaps
 *      back to currentColor, avoiding a visible snap to the starting
 *      position.
 *
 *      If you want taller/shorter bands, change y2 AND the slide
 *      distance proportionally (4 × (y1−y2)) to keep cycles-per-second
 *      flow speed constant.
 *
 * After ~3.1s the fill reverts to currentColor.
 *
 * Why SMIL: `gradientTransform` is an SVG attribute, not a CSS
 * property, so WAAPI can't touch it. SMIL's `beginElement()` gives
 * reliable click-to-trigger and clean replays on rapid clicks.
 *
 * Rapid clicks: each click clears any pending fill-reset timer,
 * restarts the SMIL animation, and queues a fresh reset. No stacking,
 * no stutter.
 *
 * Reduced motion: skips the slide entirely. Shows the static rainbow
 * for ~600ms then reverts — user still gets the color payoff without
 * horizontal motion.
 *
 * The gradient `id` is derived from `useId()` so multiple instances
 * of the component won't collide (colons stripped — some SVG
 * implementations reject them in IDs).
 */

